cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  twindiverge: attributing a twin-versus-reality residual to a parameter
  subset, after refusing the parameters the telemetry cannot identify
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-11-09
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Attributes a digital-twin-versus-reality residual to a parameter subset, and
  reports which parameters are structurally unidentifiable from the supplied
  telemetry set BEFORE attributing anything to the rest. The order of
  operations is the product: identifiability analysis gates attribution, and a
  refusal is a reported outcome rather than an error path. Baseline 1 is local
  structural and practical identifiability from the rank and conditioning of
  the noise-scaled sensitivity matrix, with Fisher information and Cramer-Rao
  bounds per parameter carrying the finiteness clause that a naive
  pseudo-inverse drops, near-singular directions reported as identifiable
  parameter COMBINATIONS rather than individual parameters, and practical
  identifiability separated from structural. Baseline 2 is first-order
  sensitivity attribution and generalised least squares on the identifiable
  subspace only, with confidence regions and with the family of perturbations
  the telemetry cannot distinguish printed beside every single-parameter
  answer. Baseline 3 is the nonlinear profile likelihood per parameter, the
  reference the analytic covariance is scored against, with a flat profile
  overriding whatever the covariance claims. A learned residual-signature
  classifier maps a residual time series to a responsible parameter, exposes a
  confidence output and no point prediction, and is scored on accuracy at a
  measured computation budget AND separately on its behaviour inside the
  unidentifiable subspace. The declared plant is a single-state reaction-wheel
  model whose rate channel cannot see the absolute torque scale, which is proved
  on paper as an exact identity and confirmed numerically: on the reference 200 s
  rate-only record the rank is 4 of 5 with 5.6483 decades of margin from the
  declared cutoff, all five parameters are refused, and the computed null space
  agrees with its derivation to a worst principal angle of 0.000000e+00 degrees
  while the identifiable subspace is measured to be the space of the four ratios
  to within 1.207418e-06 degrees. Five of five parameter profiles on that set
  are flat to 7.385097e-10 in chi-square while the linearised covariance claims
  half-widths between 0.7 and 11 per cent, so the profile overrides it five
  times out of five; where the profiles close, on a two-channel set, the
  covariance is validated against them to 0.9972, 0.9977, 1.0024 and 1.1622.
  The Cramer-Rao bound lies below the measured scatter of 20 independent
  nonlinear fits in every direction, with the linear estimator's ratios on the
  bound at 1.002398 to 1.209669. Structural rank is constant at 4 over record
  lengths from 10 s to 200 s while the bound on the worst identifiable direction
  falls by a factor of 30979.4, which is the operational content of the
  distinction between structural and practical identifiability. Validation is
  level 3, with 254 tests, 29 negative controls of which 17 must fire and all 17
  do and 12 must not and none do, and an audit finding 0 of 254 assertions
  dependent on the hidden pytest.approx absolute default. The headline result is
  the learned component's behaviour inside the unidentifiable subspace: on 120
  held-out cases whose telemetry is identical to a single-parameter fault to
  within two units in the last place, the analytic path refuses 1.000000 and
  declares the equivalence family on 1.000000, while the learned classifier
  answers with a mean confidence of 0.993426, an accuracy of 0.000000, a
  confident-and-wrong rate of 0.991667 and an ambiguity declared on 0.000000. It
  returns a confident wrong attribution exactly where the analytic method
  correctly refuses to answer. Outside that subspace the analytic attributor
  also wins, 1.000000 against 0.991667 on ordinary faults and 0.941667 against
  0.641667 on faults a tenth the size. All numbers are measured against
  synthetic records generated by the package's own declared equations; no
  measurement of any real reaction wheel, spacecraft, test article or flight
  computer appears anywhere. Research grade. Not flight-qualified, not
  certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - digital-twin
  - structural-identifiability
  - practical-identifiability
  - fisher-information
  - cramer-rao
  - profile-likelihood
  - sensitivity-analysis
  - parameter-attribution
  - reaction-wheel
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Bellman
        given-names: "R."
      - family-names: "Astrom"
        given-names: "K. J."
    title: "On structural identifiability"
    journal: "Mathematical Biosciences"
    volume: 7
    issue: "3-4"
    year: 1970
    start: 329
    end: 339
    notes: >-
      The origin of the structural-identifiability question and of the
      distinction this product's baseline 1 implements locally. Cited for the
      concept and explicitly NOT implemented: the global differential-algebra
      question is named in README.md as outside this product's scope.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Raue
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Kreutz
        given-names: "C."
      - family-names: Maiwald
        given-names: "T."
      - family-names: Bachmann
        given-names: "J."
      - family-names: Schilling
        given-names: "M."
      - family-names: "Klingmueller"
        given-names: "U."
      - family-names: Timmer
        given-names: "J."
    title: >-
      Structural and practical identifiability analysis of partially observed
      dynamical models by exploiting the profile likelihood
    journal: "Bioinformatics"
    volume: 25
    issue: 15
    year: 2009
    start: 1923
    end: 1929
    notes: >-
      The profile-likelihood method of twindiverge.profile, equation (17), and
      the distinction between structural and practical identifiability that
      baseline 1 reports as REFUSED against WEAK. The flatness of a profile as
      the operational definition of practical unidentifiability is taken from
      here; measured on this product's reference set, five of five parameter
      profiles are flat to 7.385097e-10 in chi-square.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Rao
        given-names: "C. R."
    title: >-
      Information and the accuracy attainable in the estimation of statistical
      parameters
    journal: "Bulletin of the Calcutta Mathematical Society"
    volume: 37
    year: 1945
    start: 81
    end: 91
    notes: >-
      The bound of equation (10), including the clause that makes it infinite
      for a linear functional outside the range of the Fisher information. That
      clause is the whole of this product's refusal, and the bound is checked
      against the measured scatter of 20 nonlinear fits in
      validation/validate_independent_bound.py.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: "Cramer"
        given-names: "H."
    title: "Mathematical Methods of Statistics"
    publisher:
      name: "Princeton University Press"
    year: 1946
    notes: >-
      The standard statement of the same bound, cited alongside Rao 1945.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Golub
        given-names: "G. H."
      - family-names: "Van Loan"
        given-names: "C. F."
    title: "Matrix Computations"
    edition: "4th"
    publisher:
      name: "Johns Hopkins University Press"
    year: 2013
    notes: >-
      Chapter 2 for the singular value decomposition and numerical rank, which
      is how baseline 1 decides a rank and why it uses a relative cutoff on the
      singular values; chapter 5 for least squares and conditioning, which is
      why nothing here is computed from the normal equations. The measured
      consequence is in the output: the Fisher information of the reference set
      has a condition number of 5.268523e+16 and a least eigenvalue of
      -4.684327e-08, for a matrix that is positive semi-definite by
      construction.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Dickinson
        given-names: "R. P."
      - family-names: Gelinas
        given-names: "R. J."
    title: >-
      Sensitivity analysis of ordinary differential equation systems - a direct
      method
    journal: "Journal of Computational Physics"
    volume: 21
    issue: 2
    year: 1976
    start: 123
    end: 143
    notes: >-
      The forward (direct) sensitivity method of equation (3), integrated
      alongside the state. Verified here against an independently written
      complex-step derivative to 4.304265e-15 relative.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Saltelli
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Ratto
        given-names: "M."
      - family-names: Andres
        given-names: "T."
      - family-names: Campolongo
        given-names: "F."
      - family-names: Cariboni
        given-names: "J."
      - family-names: Gatelli
        given-names: "D."
      - family-names: Saisana
        given-names: "M."
      - family-names: Tarantola
        given-names: "S."
    title: "Global Sensitivity Analysis: The Primer"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 2008
    notes: >-
      Cited for what this product does NOT do. The sensitivities here are local
      derivatives at one parameter point, not variance shares over a parameter
      distribution; README.md names SALib as the package to use for the global
      question instead.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Bard
        given-names: "Y."
    title: "Nonlinear Parameter Estimation"
    publisher:
      name: "Academic Press"
    year: 1974
    notes: >-
      Chapters 4 and 7, the Gaussian-likelihood Fisher information of a
      nonlinear regression model and the noise scaling that makes equation (8)
      dimensionless and its Gramian the information matrix of equation (9).
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Seber
        given-names: "G. A. F."
      - family-names: Wild
        given-names: "C. J."
    title: "Nonlinear Regression"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 1989
    notes: >-
      Chapter 2 for the weighted-least-squares form and its covariance, which is
      equation (12); chapter 5 for the likelihood-ratio interval and its
      asymmetry, which is why twindiverge.profile reports separate lower and
      upper limits rather than a half-width.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Butcher
        given-names: "J. C."
    title: "Numerical Methods for Ordinary Differential Equations"
    edition: "3rd"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 2016
    notes: >-
      Section 232, the classical fourth-order Runge-Kutta scheme used for the
      state and the sensitivities. Its order is measured here at 4.0201 to
      4.2425 against the closed-form solution of twindiverge.reference.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: "Armstrong-Helouvry"
        given-names: "B."
      - family-names: Dupont
        given-names: "P."
      - family-names: "Canudas de Wit"
        given-names: "C."
    title: >-
      A survey of models, analysis tools and compensation methods for the
      control of machines with friction
    journal: "Automatica"
    volume: 30
    issue: 7
    year: 1994
    start: 1083
    end: 1138
    notes: >-
      Section 2, the Coulomb-plus-viscous friction decomposition whose FORM
      equation (1) takes. The coefficient values used here are declared and are
      not taken from this source.
  - type: conference-paper
    authors:
      - family-names: Bialke
        given-names: "B."
    title: >-
      High fidelity mathematical modeling of reaction wheel performance
    collection-title: >-
      21st Annual AAS Guidance and Control Conference, AAS 98-063
    year: 1998
    notes: >-
      The same friction decomposition applied to a spacecraft reaction wheel,
      together with the motor torque constant of equation (1). Again the form is
      taken and the coefficient values are not: they are declared, and are a
      fiction with an engineering shape.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Clopper
        given-names: "C. J."
      - family-names: Pearson
        given-names: "E. S."
    title: >-
      The use of confidence or fiducial limits illustrated in the case of the
      binomial
    journal: "Biometrika"
    volume: 26
    issue: 4
    year: 1934
    start: 404
    end: 413
    notes: >-
      The exact binomial interval attached to every proportion reported here.
      Used rather than a normal approximation because the proportions reach 0
      and 1: an accuracy of 120 out of 120 has a normal standard error of
      exactly zero.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Brier
        given-names: "G. W."
    title: "Verification of forecasts expressed in terms of probability"
    journal: "Monthly Weather Review"
    volume: 78
    issue: 1
    year: 1950
    start: 1
    end: 3
    notes: >-
      The multi-class score used to measure the learned component's
      calibration. Measured 0.006568 where the question is well posed and
      0.993340 inside the unidentifiable subspace.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Friedman
        given-names: "J. H."
    title: "Greedy function approximation: a gradient boosting machine"
    journal: "Annals of Statistics"
    volume: 29
    issue: 5
    year: 2001
    start: 1189
    end: 1232
    notes: >-
      The gradient boosting algorithm behind
      sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingClassifier, which fits the learned
      component. Its measured accuracy of 0.000000 at a mean confidence of
      0.993426 inside the unidentifiable subspace is this product's headline
      result.
